            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>The Concept of Fixed Action Patterns</strong><br>\nLorenz introduced the concept of fixed action patterns, which are pre-programmed, instinctive behaviors triggered by specific stimuli. He explained that these patterns are species-specific and can be observed across individuals within the same species. Fixed action patterns play a crucial role in survival and reproductive behaviors, which Lorenz illustrated through various experiments and observations.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Nobel Prize and Recognition</strong><br>\nIn 1973, Konrad Lorenz, along with Nikolaas Tinbergen and Karl von Frisch, was awarded the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine for their discoveries concerning organization and elicitation of individual and social behavior patterns. This recognition cemented Lorenz's position as a leading figure in the field of animal behavior and acknowledged the significance of ethology as a science.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Books and Publications</strong><br>\nLorenz was not only a researcher but also a prolific writer. His books, such as \"King Solomon's Ring\" and \"On Aggression,\" brought ethological concepts to a wider audience. His writing style made complex scientific ideas accessible and entertaining, helping to raise public interest in animal behavior and conservation issues.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
