          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Personal Philosophies and Controversies</strong><br>\nThroughout his career, Lorenz's work sparked discussions on the influence of genetics on behavior. His views on aggression and his interpretations of animal behavior in terms of natural selection and evolutionary advantage were both influential and, at times, controversial. Lorenz's ideas contributed to debates on human sociology and psychology, though some of his theories have been critiqued over time.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Later Life and Legacy</strong><br>\nIn his later years, Lorenz continued to write and lecture on behavioral science and philosophy. He remained active in his research until his death on February 27, 1989. Lorenz's legacy is evident in the ongoing work of ethologists and the continued relevance of his research in understanding animal and human behavior.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Impact on Modern Science</strong><br>\nKonrad Lorenz's influence extends beyond ethology into various fields including psychology, ecology, and evolutionary biology. His insights into animal behavior have provided a foundation for further research on the genetic and environmental factors that shape behavioral patterns. Today, Lorenz is remembered for his contribution to our understanding of the natural world and the behavior of the creatures within it.</p>\n"
              },
